Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Adapted from the story by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Public domain text (Project Gutenberg eBook #46)
Draft: Short film pre-production

==

FADE IN:

A black card.

Title: MARLEY WAS DEAD: TO BEGIN WITH.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

Fog presses the windows. Candles smear the brown air.

EBENEZER SCROOGE, sharp as flint, works at his stool beside a very small fire. Beyond an open door, in a dismal little cell like a tank, BOB CRATCHIT copies letters at a fire that looks like one coal. Cratchit wears a white comforter.

On the warehouse door, still lettered: SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

City clocks have gone three; it is already dark.

SCROOGE'S NEPHEW enters in a glow from rapid walking, face ruddy, eyes bright.

NEPHEW
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

NEPHEW
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

NEPHEW
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

Scrooge answers only with another Bah and Humbug. The nephew speaks of Christmas as a kind, forgiving, charitable time. In the Tank, Cratchit involuntarily applauds—then pokes out his last spark.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

NEPHEW
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

Scrooge refuses him utterly. The nephew leaves without an angry word and wishes Cratchit joy of the season at the outer door. Cratchit returns it cordially.

Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers, hats off.

GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute...

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid, from what you said at first, that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it.

They ask what to put him down for.

SCROOGE
Nothing!

GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. ... Those who are badly off must go there.

GENTLEMAN
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. ... It's not my business. ... Good afternoon, gentlemen!

They withdraw. Later, at shutting time:

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. ... But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Cratchit promises, snuffs his candle, and goes—comforter dangling, no great-coat.

EXT. YARD BEFORE SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A lowering pile up a dark yard. Fog and frost on the black gateway.

Scrooge puts his key to the door.

The large knocker is MARLEY'S FACE—livid, spectacles on a ghostly forehead, hair stirred, eyes wide and motionless.

Then it is a knocker again.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - CONTINUOUS

Scrooge lights a candle, checks sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room. Nobody. A little saucepan of gruel upon the hob.

He double-locks himself in—not his custom—puts on dressing-gown, slippers, nightcap, and sits close to a very low fire among quaint Dutch tiles.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

A disused bell begins to swing—softly, then loudly. Every bell in the house answers. They cease together.

A clanking, as of a heavy chain dragged over casks below. The sound comes up the stairs, straight toward his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

Without a pause the apparition passes through the heavy door. The dying flame leaps up.

JACOB MARLEY'S GHOST: pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots; transparent; a chain about his middle of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, heavy steel purses. A folded kerchief binds his head and chin.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

Scrooge doubts his senses—gravy more than grave. The Ghost raises a frightful cry, shakes its chain; removes the bandage; its lower jaw drops upon its breast.

Scrooge falls upon his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
Man of the worldly mind! do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen... and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard... Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge trembles.

MARLEY
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? ... You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

MARLEY (CONT'D)
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

MARLEY (CONT'D)
I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. ... You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. ... Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds its head again. The window rises by itself. Outside: phantoms in chains, restless, moaning, powerless to help.

Marley floats out upon the bleak night. Scrooge closes the window, tries the double-locked door, fails even to finish the word Humbug, and falls asleep upon the instant.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT (THE BELL TOLLS ONE)

Darkness. Church chimes. A deep, dull, hollow ONE.

Light flashes. Bed-curtains are drawn aside by a hand.

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST: childlike and old at once; white hair; tender bloom; white tunic; summer flowers; holly branch; a bright jet of light from its head; extinguisher-cap under its arm. Its form fluctuates.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. ... Your welfare! ... Your reclamation, then. Take heed! Rise! and walk with me!

It lays a hand upon his heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. OPEN COUNTRY ROAD - WINTER DAY (VISION)

Snow. Clear cold. The city gone.

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

They come to a dull red-brick school of broken fortunes. In a long bare room, a LONELY BOY reads near a feeble fire.

Present Scrooge weeps to see his poor forgotten self.

A little girl—FAN—darts in and embraces the boy.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, home, home! ... Father is so much kinder than he used to be... and you're to be a man! ... first, we're to be together all the Christmas long...

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature... But she had a large heart! ... She died a woman... and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Another shadow: YOUNG SCROOGE in prime of life beside a fair young woman in mourning-dress—BELLE. Tears in her eyes.

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me... A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty; and there is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
You fear the world too much. ... I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. ... I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

She leaves him.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

He seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down. Light still streams from under it. He reels back into his own bedroom and heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT (AGAIN THE STROKE OF ONE)

A blaze of ruddy light from the adjoining room. A strange voice calls him by name.

INT. TRANSFORMED SITTING-ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Walls hung with living green; holly, mistletoe, ivy; a mighty blaze; heaped foods like a throne.

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—a jolly Giant in a green robe bordered with white fur; bare breast and feet; holly wreath set with icicles; torch like Plenty's horn.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! ... I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. ... To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING (VISION)

Snow scraped from roofs; cheerful bustle despite gloom. The Spirit sprinkles dinners from his torch.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY (VISION)

Four rooms, poor and brave. MRS. CRATCHIT, twice-turned gown brave in ribbons, lays the cloth. Children throng.

BOB CRATCHIT enters with at least three feet of comforter, threadbare clothes brushed, and TINY TIM upon his shoulder—little crutch, iron frame on his limbs.

They feast on goose and a pudding like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing with brandy and holly.

BOB
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL
(echoing)

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim sits close to his father. Bob holds his withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head, overcome with penitence.

Bob toasts Mr. Scrooge as Founder of the Feast. Mrs. Cratchit bristles, then drinks for Bob's sake and the Day's. The name casts a shadow—then merriment returns.

Later, from the Spirit's robe: two children, wretched—Ignorance and Want.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both... but most of all beware this boy...

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? ... Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Giant is gone.

A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, comes like a mist along the ground—one outstretched hand visible.

EXT. CITY / THE EXCHANGE - DAY (VISION)

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME points. Merchants talk lightly of a death—cheap funeral, no one to go, money unknown. No tenderness.

INT. RAG-AND-BOTTLE SHOP - NIGHT (VISION)

Old iron, rags, bones. A charwoman, a laundress, an undertaker's man sell plunder from a dead man's rooms—bed-curtains, blankets, the shirt off his corpse—laughing.

Scrooge recoils in disgust.

INT. DARK BEDROOM - NIGHT (VISION)

A bare uncurtained bed. Something covered with a ragged sheet—unwatched, unwept, uncared for. A cat at the door; rats beneath the hearth.

The Phantom points to the head. Scrooge cannot lift the cover.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY (VISION)

Quiet. Very quiet. The family sews and sits still. Bob returns from the green burying-ground, breaks down for his little child, then steadies—kindness remembered, patience vowed.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY (VISION)

Walled in; grass and weeds; choked with too much burying.

The Spirit points down to one neglected grave.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The hand still points.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends... But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

Scrooge creeps forward and reads upon the stone:

EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? ... No, Spirit! Oh no, no! ... Why show me this, if I am past all hope! ... I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. ... Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He clutches the spectral hand. The Phantom shrinks, collapses, dwindles into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

The bedpost is his own. The bed, the room—his own. Time before him, to make amends.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

He laughs and cries at once—frisks into the sitting-room.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. ... A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world!

Church bells peal—clash, clang, hammer—glorious.

He opens the window on clear, bright, jovial cold. Golden sunlight.

Below: a boy in Sunday clothes.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
What's to-day?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. ... Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? ... the prize Turkey... the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
Go and buy it. ... Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
(whispering, delighted)
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it.

He pats the knocker he once feared.

EXT. LONDON STREETS - DAY

Scrooge, dressed all in his best, regards everyone with a delighted smile. He meets the portly gentleman of yesterday, begs pardon, and whispers a munificent pledge.

GENTLEMAN
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. ... Will you come and see me?

GENTLEMAN
I will!

INT. NEPHEW'S DINING-ROOM - AFTERNOON

Scrooge sidles his face round the door.

SCROOGE
Fred! ... It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Nothing could be heartier. Wonderful happiness.

INT. COUNTING-HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

Scrooge early, door wide, hoping to catch Bob late. Bob arrives a full eighteen minutes and a half behind, hat and comforter off, to his stool in a jiffy.

SCROOGE
(feigning the old growl)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. ... It's only once a year, sir. ... I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore...

He leaps, digs Bob in the waistcoat—then claps him on the back.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you, for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

EXT. CITY - MONTAGE

Scrooge better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he is a second father. He becomes as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew.

TINY TIM (V.O.)
God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

END.
